{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b1a288fce7cf5c38", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# CodecSlime: Temporal Redundancy Compression of Neural Speech Codec via Dynamic Frame Rate\narXiv: 2506.21074\nAuthors: Hankun Wang, Yiwei Guo, Chongtian Shao, Bohan Li, Kai Yu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of CodecSlime, a plugin-style, architecture-agnostic, and fully unsupervised method that enables dynamic frame rate (DFR) for fixed-frame-rate neural speech codecs to compress temporal redundancy.\n  2. Development of ScheDFR, an inference-time technique that utilizes dynamic programming to adaptively merge similar adjacent frames based on a feature-space distortion surrogate objective.\n  3. Proposal of Melt-and-Cool, a two-stage training recipe that adapts fixed-frame-rate codec backbones to handle dynamic frame rates through random downsampling post-training and optimal scheduling fine-tuning.\n  4. Demonstration of significant reconstruction quality improvements, achieving up to a 32% relative Word Error Rate reduction at 40 Hz compared to fixed-frame-rate baselines with similar bitrates.\n  5. Enabling of flexible trade-offs between reconstruction quality and bitrate, allowing a single trained model to support and outperform fixed-frame-rate models at multiple inference frame rates.\n\nTags: 压缩/量化-VQ, 压缩/量化-FSQ, 压缩/监督-无监督, 压缩/语义声学-声学\n===NEXT===\n# DisCo-Speech: Controllable Zero-Shot Speech Generation with A Disentangled Speech Codec\narXiv: 2512.13251\nAuthors: Tao Li, Wenshuo Ge, Zhichao Wang, Zihao Cui, Yong Ma, Yingying Gao, Chao Deng, Shilei Zhang, Junlan Feng\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of DisCodec, a disentangled speech codec that explicitly factorizes speech into content, prosody, and timbre subspaces using parallel encoders and hybrid losses.\n  2. Development of a two-stage training paradigm that first achieves tri-factor disentanglement and then fuses content and prosody into unified, timbre-agnostic tokens while jointly optimizing reconstruction to mitigate the disentanglement-reconstruction trade-off.\n  3. Design of a zero-shot controllable TTS framework where a standard language model performs prosodic continuation and a decoder injects target timbre, enabling flexible independent control.\n  4. Demonstration of competitive voice cloning and superior zero-shot prosody control compared to existing state-of-the-art systems.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/控制-参考语音, 压缩, 压缩/量化-FSQ, 压缩/语义声学-语义, 压缩/语义声学-声学", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "058a4cad-00f1-423a-87d6-78b4fbfd35a6", "uuid": "a6222cbc-7862-41ea-9624-aca8c02c7fad", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T07:46:52.768Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# CodecSlime: Temporal Redundancy Compression of Neural Speech Codec via Dynamic Frame Rate\narXiv: 2506.21074\nAuthors: Hankun Wang, Yiwei Guo, Chongtian Shao, Bohan Li, Kai Yu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of CodecSlime, a plugin-style, architecture-agnostic, and fully unsupervised method that enables dynamic frame rate (DFR) for fixed-frame-rate neural speech codecs to compress temporal redundancy.\n  2. Development of ScheDFR, an inference-time technique that utilizes dynamic programming to adaptively merge similar adjacent frames based on a feature-space distortion surrogate objective.\n  3. Proposal of Melt-and-Cool, a two-stage training recipe that adapts fixed-frame-rate codec backbones to handle dynamic frame rates through random downsampling post-training and optimal scheduling fine-tuning.\n  4. Demonstration of significant reconstruction quality improvements, achieving up to a 32% relative Word Error Rate reduction at 40 Hz compared to fixed-frame-rate baselines with similar bitrates.\n  5. Enabling of flexible trade-offs between reconstruction quality and bitrate, allowing a single trained model to support and outperform fixed-frame-rate models at multiple inference frame rates.\n\nTags: 压缩/量化-VQ, 压缩/量化-FSQ, 压缩/监督-无监督, 压缩/语义声学-声学\n===NEXT===\n# DisCo-Speech: Controllable Zero-Shot Speech Generation with A Disentangled Speech Codec\narXiv: 2512.13251\nAuthors: Tao Li, Wenshuo Ge, Zhichao Wang, Zihao Cui, Yong Ma, Yingying Gao, Chao Deng, Shilei Zhang, Junlan Feng\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of DisCodec, a disentangled speech codec that explicitly factorizes speech into content, prosody, and timbre subspaces using parallel encoders and hybrid losses.\n  2. Development of a two-stage training paradigm that first achieves tri-factor disentanglement and then fuses content and prosody into unified, timbre-agnostic tokens while jointly optimizing reconstruction to mitigate the disentanglement-reconstruction trade-off.\n  3. Design of a zero-shot controllable TTS framework where a standard language model performs prosodic continuation and a decoder injects target timbre, enabling flexible independent control.\n  4. Demonstration of competitive voice cloning and superior zero-shot prosody control compared to existing state-of-the-art systems.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/控制-参考语音, 压缩, 压缩/量化-FSQ, 压缩/语义声学-语义, 压缩/语义声学-声学", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8d96c515cb76e7f1", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# FELLE: Autoregressive Speech Synthesis with Token-Wise Coarse-to-Fine Flow Matching\narXiv: 2502.11128\nAuthors: Hui Wang, Shujie Liu, Lingwei Meng, Jinyu Li, Yifan Yang, Shiwan Zhao, Haiyang Sun, Yanqing Liu, Haoqin Sun, Jiaming Zhou, Yan Lu, Yong Qin\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose an AR speech synthesis framework leveraging token-wise flow matching for continuous speech modeling, eliminating restrictive distribution assumptions while preserving speech signals' multimodal characteristics.\n  2. We design a dynamic prior mechanism that modifies the vanilla prior distribution in flow matching by incorporating information from the previous step, improving coherence and stability.\n  3. We introduce a coarse-to-fine flow matching architecture that explicitly captures inter-frequency correlations through multi-stage spectral refinement, achieving significant improvements in mel-spectrogram generation.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow\n===NEXT===\n# Autoregressive Speech Synthesis without Vector Quantization\narXiv: 2407.08551\nAuthors: Lingwei Meng, Long Zhou, Shujie Liu, Sanyuan Chen, Bing Han, Shujie Hu, Yanqing Liu, Jinyu Li, Sheng Zhao, Xixin Wu, Helen Meng, Furu Wei\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose MELLE, a novel continuous-valued token based language modeling approach for text-to-speech synthesis that bypasses the need for vector quantization.\n  2. We apply regression loss with a proposed spectrogram flux loss function instead of cross-entropy loss to model the probability distribution of continuous-valued tokens.\n  3. We incorporate variational inference into the model to facilitate sampling mechanisms, thereby enhancing output diversity and model robustness.\n  4. We demonstrate that the single-stage MELLE mitigates robustness issues inherent in sampling vector-quantized codes, achieves superior performance across multiple metrics, and offers a more streamlined paradigm compared to two-stage codec language models.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "058a4cad-00f1-423a-87d6-78b4fbfd35a6", "uuid": "32d86c96-4db1-4a56-9679-3764549069e1", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T07:46:53.282Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# FELLE: Autoregressive Speech Synthesis with Token-Wise Coarse-to-Fine Flow Matching\narXiv: 2502.11128\nAuthors: Hui Wang, Shujie Liu, Lingwei Meng, Jinyu Li, Yifan Yang, Shiwan Zhao, Haiyang Sun, Yanqing Liu, Haoqin Sun, Jiaming Zhou, Yan Lu, Yong Qin\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose an AR speech synthesis framework leveraging token-wise flow matching for continuous speech modeling, eliminating restrictive distribution assumptions while preserving speech signals' multimodal characteristics.\n  2. We design a dynamic prior mechanism that modifies the vanilla prior distribution in flow matching by incorporating information from the previous step, improving coherence and stability.\n  3. We introduce a coarse-to-fine flow matching architecture that explicitly captures inter-frequency correlations through multi-stage spectral refinement, achieving significant improvements in mel-spectrogram generation.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow\n===NEXT===\n# Autoregressive Speech Synthesis without Vector Quantization\narXiv: 2407.08551\nAuthors: Lingwei Meng, Long Zhou, Shujie Liu, Sanyuan Chen, Bing Han, Shujie Hu, Yanqing Liu, Jinyu Li, Sheng Zhao, Xixin Wu, Helen Meng, Furu Wei\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose MELLE, a novel continuous-valued token based language modeling approach for text-to-speech synthesis that bypasses the need for vector quantization.\n  2. We apply regression loss with a proposed spectrogram flux loss function instead of cross-entropy loss to model the probability distribution of continuous-valued tokens.\n  3. We incorporate variational inference into the model to facilitate sampling mechanisms, thereby enhancing output diversity and model robustness.\n  4. We demonstrate that the single-stage MELLE mitigates robustness issues inherent in sampling vector-quantized codes, achieves superior performance across multiple metrics, and offers a more streamlined paradigm compared to two-stage codec language models.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b7b03036f96f2686", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# Robust and Efficient Autoregressive Speech Synthesis with Dynamic Chunk-wise Prediction Policy\narXiv: 2506.22023\nAuthors: Bohan Li, Zhihan Li, Haoran Wang, Hanglei Zhang, Yiwei Guo, Hankun Wang, Xie Chen, Kai Yu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of a dynamic chunk-wise autoregressive synthesis framework (DCAR) that utilizes a chunk-to-frame attention mechanism trained with multi-token prediction to dynamically adjust token prediction spans.\n  2. Significant reduction of sequence length dependency in autoregressive speech synthesis while maintaining high generation quality.\n  3. Achievement of up to 72.27% improvement in intelligibility and 2.61× inference speedup simultaneously compared to traditional next-token prediction models.\n  4. Development of a lightweight on-policy module (DCPO) that effectively optimizes the dynamic chunk prediction policy for variable speech contexts.\n  5. Demonstration of the method's versatility and scalability across different tokenizers, large-scale datasets, and open-source TTS frameworks like CosyVoice.\n\nTags: TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/功能-RL优化, TTS/功能-快速生成\n===NEXT===\n# UniVoice: Unifying Autoregressive ASR and Flow-Matching based TTS with Large Language Models\narXiv: 2510.04593\nAuthors: Wenhao Guan, Zhikang Niu, Ziyue Jiang, Kaidi Wang, Peijie Chen, Qingyang Hong, Lin Li, Xie Chen\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We present a unified framework that integrates autoregressive speech recognition with flow-matching-based synthesis in pre-trained LLMs, enabling joint speech understanding and generation within a single model.\n  2. We propose a dual-attention mechanism that switches between causal masking for recognition and bidirectional attention for synthesis, along with text-prefix guided infilling for high-fidelity zero-shot voice cloning.\n  3. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our unified approach matches or surpasses state-of-the-art specialized models in both speech recognition and zero-shot speech synthesis performance, while maintaining parameter efficiency.\n\nTags: TTS, ASR, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow, TTS/控制-参考语音", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "058a4cad-00f1-423a-87d6-78b4fbfd35a6", "uuid": "014d3bad-61e5-42ef-9481-8344e9637cc3", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T07:46:53.797Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# Robust and Efficient Autoregressive Speech Synthesis with Dynamic Chunk-wise Prediction Policy\narXiv: 2506.22023\nAuthors: Bohan Li, Zhihan Li, Haoran Wang, Hanglei Zhang, Yiwei Guo, Hankun Wang, Xie Chen, Kai Yu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of a dynamic chunk-wise autoregressive synthesis framework (DCAR) that utilizes a chunk-to-frame attention mechanism trained with multi-token prediction to dynamically adjust token prediction spans.\n  2. Significant reduction of sequence length dependency in autoregressive speech synthesis while maintaining high generation quality.\n  3. Achievement of up to 72.27% improvement in intelligibility and 2.61× inference speedup simultaneously compared to traditional next-token prediction models.\n  4. Development of a lightweight on-policy module (DCPO) that effectively optimizes the dynamic chunk prediction policy for variable speech contexts.\n  5. Demonstration of the method's versatility and scalability across different tokenizers, large-scale datasets, and open-source TTS frameworks like CosyVoice.\n\nTags: TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/功能-RL优化, TTS/功能-快速生成\n===NEXT===\n# UniVoice: Unifying Autoregressive ASR and Flow-Matching based TTS with Large Language Models\narXiv: 2510.04593\nAuthors: Wenhao Guan, Zhikang Niu, Ziyue Jiang, Kaidi Wang, Peijie Chen, Qingyang Hong, Lin Li, Xie Chen\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We present a unified framework that integrates autoregressive speech recognition with flow-matching-based synthesis in pre-trained LLMs, enabling joint speech understanding and generation within a single model.\n  2. We propose a dual-attention mechanism that switches between causal masking for recognition and bidirectional attention for synthesis, along with text-prefix guided infilling for high-fidelity zero-shot voice cloning.\n  3. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our unified approach matches or surpasses state-of-the-art specialized models in both speech recognition and zero-shot speech synthesis performance, while maintaining parameter efficiency.\n\nTags: TTS, ASR, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow, TTS/控制-参考语音", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-87a263319a57dd2b", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# Latent Speech-Text Transformer\narXiv: 2510.06195\nAuthors: Yen-Ju Lu, Yashesh Gaur, Wei Zhou, Benjamin Muller, Jesus Villalba, Najim Dehak, Luke Zettlemoyer, Gargi Ghosh, Mike Lewis, Srinivasan Iyer, Duc Le\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. LST improves speech and text performance in both data- and compute-controlled settings compared to prior interleaved speech-text models, while reducing training and inference compute.\n  2. The paper introduces latent speech patching as a unified mechanism to compress autoregressive speech sequences, and analyzes static, alignment-based, and curriculum patching strategies.\n  3. LST benefits persist and grow with model scale from 1B to 7B parameters, demonstrating improved sample efficiency and more favorable compute-optimal scaling behavior.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型-交错标记, LALM/潜在表示\n===NEXT===\n# SemaVoice: Semantic-Aware Continuous Autoregressive Speech Synthesis\narXiv: 2605.16964\nAuthors: Huimeng Wang, Hui Lu, Jiajun Deng, Haoning Xu, Youjun Chen, Xueyuan Chen, Zhaoqing Li, Shuhai Peng, Shiyin Kang, Xunying Liu\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We present SemaVoice, a semantic-aware continuous autoregressive modeling framework that enables effective unified modeling of high-level semantic-prosodic planning and fine-grained acoustic rendering for high-fidelity zero-shot TTS.\n  2. We identify the mismatch between reconstruction-driven continuous speech representations and semantic-prosodic modeling as a key bottleneck in continuous autoregressive TTS, and introduce an SFM-guided alignment mechanism that enhances semantic coherence by aligning continuous representations with high-level semantic and prosodic information encoded in speech foundation models, improving generation robustness without modifying the downstream TTS architecture or training pipeline.\n  3. We validate SemaVoice through large-scale training on 150K hours of bilingual speech, achieving competitive performance compared with state-of-the-art open-source zero-shot TTS systems in both objective and subjective evaluations.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/架构-NAR-Diffusion", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "058a4cad-00f1-423a-87d6-78b4fbfd35a6", "uuid": "f99bcacd-ca03-408a-a8cd-c3a6535abd3f", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T07:46:54.320Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# Latent Speech-Text Transformer\narXiv: 2510.06195\nAuthors: Yen-Ju Lu, Yashesh Gaur, Wei Zhou, Benjamin Muller, Jesus Villalba, Najim Dehak, Luke Zettlemoyer, Gargi Ghosh, Mike Lewis, Srinivasan Iyer, Duc Le\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. LST improves speech and text performance in both data- and compute-controlled settings compared to prior interleaved speech-text models, while reducing training and inference compute.\n  2. The paper introduces latent speech patching as a unified mechanism to compress autoregressive speech sequences, and analyzes static, alignment-based, and curriculum patching strategies.\n  3. LST benefits persist and grow with model scale from 1B to 7B parameters, demonstrating improved sample efficiency and more favorable compute-optimal scaling behavior.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型-交错标记, LALM/潜在表示\n===NEXT===\n# SemaVoice: Semantic-Aware Continuous Autoregressive Speech Synthesis\narXiv: 2605.16964\nAuthors: Huimeng Wang, Hui Lu, Jiajun Deng, Haoning Xu, Youjun Chen, Xueyuan Chen, Zhaoqing Li, Shuhai Peng, Shiyin Kang, Xunying Liu\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We present SemaVoice, a semantic-aware continuous autoregressive modeling framework that enables effective unified modeling of high-level semantic-prosodic planning and fine-grained acoustic rendering for high-fidelity zero-shot TTS.\n  2. We identify the mismatch between reconstruction-driven continuous speech representations and semantic-prosodic modeling as a key bottleneck in continuous autoregressive TTS, and introduce an SFM-guided alignment mechanism that enhances semantic coherence by aligning continuous representations with high-level semantic and prosodic information encoded in speech foundation models, improving generation robustness without modifying the downstream TTS architecture or training pipeline.\n  3. We validate SemaVoice through large-scale training on 150K hours of bilingual speech, achieving competitive performance compared with state-of-the-art open-source zero-shot TTS systems in both objective and subjective evaluations.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/架构-NAR-Diffusion", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785743225064", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "太好了 —— 我在 paper-wiki 中找到了非常相关的工作。关键发现：\n- **MELLE** (2407.08551)：不带 VQ 的自回归（AR）语音合成 —— 连续 token，基于回归\n- **FELLE** (2502.11128)：用于连续 AR 语音的 Token 级粗到细流匹配（flow matching）\n- **SemaVoice** (2605.16964)：语义感知连续 AR 语音合成\n- **BigCodec** (2409.05377)：带有单码本的低帧率神经编解码器\n- **CodecSlime** (2506.21074)：神经编解码器的动态帧率\n\n现在，我将使用 free-search 来寻找目标 SOTA 和最新的 ELD 相关工作。"}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "058a4cad-00f1-423a-87d6-78b4fbfd35a6", "uuid": "379904be-792c-45a1-8ee0-fe2fe86f3b28"}
